WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
Glad you're here. Welcome to Models and Agents episode 72. It's June 6th, 2026. Plenty of model

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.120
releases and agent benchmarks moving today. Microsoft just gained the freedom to build its

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.540
own frontier models after a contract changed with OpenAI, and the first MAI family is already shipping.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.220
Microsoft announced seven in-house MAI models that cover reasoning, code, image generation,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:31.720
transcription, and voice. The models were trained from scratch on licensed data with no distillation

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.860
from other labs. A large wave of open-weight releases also arrived this week across large language

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:44.600
models, vision language models, text-to-speech, and world models. Builders are watching how fast the

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:51.000
new MAI models reach competitive levels on agentic and coding tasks now that Microsoft can iterate on

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.740
its own. Microsoft revealed that a contract change roughly six months ago lifted prior

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:02.520
restrictions on its AI superintelligence team. The team can now pursue superintelligence using

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:09.480
its own researchers, data, and custom silicon. The first MAI family includes the 35 billion

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:16.480
active parameter MAI thinking one reasoning model, plus specialized models for code, image generation,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.220
transcription across 43 languages, and multilingual voice. All models were trained from scratch

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.940
on commercially licensed data. They are available today through Microsoft Foundry with third-party

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:35.580
weight tuning supported on OpenRouter, fireworks, and base 10. Enterprise customers can already use

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:41.100
frontier tuning to customize the models inside governed environments. The move shows Microsoft

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.940
building a vertically integrated stack while keeping its open AI partnership in place.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.980
On the frontier models track, this development adds a new independent player that can set its own

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:58.640
release cadence and cost trajectory. Anthropic release new research showing that Claude Opus 4.7

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:05.660
matches or exceeds dedicated NMR spectroscopy software on molecular structure interpretation tasks.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:12.400
The work focuses on helping chemists understand molecular structures from NMR data before manipulating

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:19.920
the molecules. Builders working on scientific tooling can now test whether Opus 4.7 can replace or

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.220
augment existing NMR analysis pipelines without custom fine tuning. Deepseek V4 Flash is now

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:34.740
runnable via an early llama.cpp pull request. Early users report strong intelligence for its size,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:43.040
along with native FP4 to FP8 hybrid quantization that holds up well. The model targets the 80-140

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.300
gigabyte local inference space. Custom three-bit quants created to match the original tensor layout

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.620
show reliable correctness despite slower speeds and missing GPU and FAS support.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:00.820
This release continues the open-weight model's arc where families like Deepseek keep narrowing the

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:07.360
performance gap with closed frontier systems. Google ship Gemma 4.12b as a fully open dense

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:14.740
any-to-any model. It supports text, image, audio, and video with 256-kele on context and coverage

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:22.800
of 140-plus languages. Quantization-aware training variants of the Gemma 4 family deliver speed

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.060
and VRAM gains on AMD hardware with no measurable quality loss versus standard quants.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:38.540
Nvidia released Nemotron 3 Ultra, a 550-billion hybrid Mamba MOE model with 55-billion active parameters.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:48.980
It offers 1 million context and a reported MMLU of 89.1. An NVFP4 variant claims roughly 5 times

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:55.500
throughput on blackwell hardware and is described as the first openly weighted 550-billion hybrid

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:03.160
Mamba transformer. Alibaba position twin 3.7-plus as a multimodal model built to function as a

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:10.480
full autonomous agent rather than a chat interface. The release emphasizes agentic capabilities across

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.360
modalities. Developers exploring agent frameworks should test whether the model's native agent

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:21.620
behaviors reduce the need for heavy scaffolding compared with prior twin releases.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.920
This sits squarely in the agents and tool use track where the open question remains how reliably

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:36.300
long horizon agents perform without extensive scaffolding. RedNote opensource dots dot tts, a 2-billion

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:43.900
parameter continuous text-to-speech model under Apache 2.0. It performs direct text-to-speech at 48

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:49.860
kilohertz with zero shot voice cloning and skips the traditional phoneme pipeline entirely.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:56.680
Teams building local voice agents now have a fully open continuous alternative to codec-based tts

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:03.680
stacks. A new modular agent called OpenLumara was released with a roughly 4k token default system

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:10.700
prompt and full modularity down to core features. It includes built-in security controls such as sandboxed

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.380
shell access and HTTP black and white lists. The project is designed for local models.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:22.440
Runs efficiently on modest hardware carries a gpl2 license and is available on github.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:30.980
Simon Willison released MicroPython WASMs, an alpha-pi-pi package that runs MicroPython inside a

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:37.000
WebAssembly sandbox. It enforces memory and CPU limits, controlled host function access,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:43.460
and no file system or network access by default. A CLI mode lets users try it immediately

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:51.820
with the command UVX MicroPython WASM-C Print Hello World. The package targets safe plug-in

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:58.600
execution inside Python applications such as data set. Users running Gemma 4 models on an AMD

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:07.060
7900 XTX reported that quantization-aware training versions deliver 1.3 to 1.5 times speed-ups

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:15.580
and meaningful VRAM savings versus standard quants. The 12B QAT variant cut generation time by 45%

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:22.280
with identical constraint following behavior on long context and creative tasks. A user running

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:32.820
twin 3.627b on an RTX 5,060 Ti found that enabling the NKVO flag to keep KV cache in RAM allowed

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:40.740
the full model to stay on GPU with F16 cache precision. The approach trades a modest tokens per second drop

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:48.120
for higher context capacity and better cache quality than quantized KV. It proved useful when fitting

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:54.800
larger context windows without dropping layers. Okay, let's pop the hood on KV cache quantization.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:07:01.880
Everyone talks about it as a simple turn it on and save VRAM toggle. In practice it is a precision

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.840
versus throughput negotiation that depends on model architecture, context length, and downstream

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:15.020
task. Attention keys and values are not equally sensitive to quantization. Value vectors often

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:20.400
tolerate lower precision better than keys because they are summed rather than used for similarity

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:27.420
lookups. Lower bit KV formats therefore introduce small per token errors that accumulate across long

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:35.560
contexts. That is why some teams see reasoning degradation only after 32k to 64k tokens even when

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:42.740
perplexity looks acceptable. New methods such as KVRN attempt to close this gap by learning per channel

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:48.740
scales that preserve the distribution of important value dimensions. They deliver Q5-level

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:57.920
KLD at roughly 4-bit storage in early lama.cpp tests. Aggressive KV quantization can still hurt

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:04.160
multi-step agent trajectories more than single-turned-chat because small embedding drift compounds when the

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:11.460
model must maintain consistent state across tool calls. When you are running agents with 50k plus context

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:18.040
or long horizon planning keep at least 5-6 bits on the value cache unless you have measured task

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:24.760
specific tolerance. For shorter chat workloads the memory win is usually worth it. This engineering

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:30.740
detail directly informs the reasoning models track where cost versus benefit remains an open question.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:38.480
Install MicroPython WASM via UVX and test a simple sandbox script to see whether it meets your plugin

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:46.600
or data transformation needs without full python privileges. Run the early deep seek V4 Flash Lama.cpp

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:52.420
build on a model you already used for coding or reasoning and compare output stability against

00:08:52.420 --> 00:09:01.000
your current quant. Test Gemma 412b QAT versus the standard Q4KM quant on your longest context workflow

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.740
to measure the real speed and VRAM difference on your hardware.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:12.700
Tridots.tts2b for any local voice agent project that needs zero-shot cloning without a separate phoneme

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:19.120
step. More labs are expected to follow red notes continuous TTS approach now that a fully

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.440
open non-codec pipeline is available. Microsoft's frontier tuning environment will likely see

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:31.400
additional enterprise case studies as more customers test MAI models on proprietary workflows.

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:41.660
Further Lama.cpp and VLLM support for the new hybrid Mamba Moe and Sparse Moe models should arrive

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:47.320
as the current PR is mature. Tomorrow keep an eye on additional open-weight audio and video

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:53.140
model releases following this week's cluster. That's models and agents for today. If you found

